  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/6/62/Tales_of_Suspense_Vol_1_47.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Vital joints of army tanks are suddenly melted away, and Tony Stark is quickly brought in since his plants <i>built</i> the tanks.&nbsp; The Melter waits for Stark to arrive and inspect, and then ambushes him.&nbsp; He's after both the army and Stark, his \"old rival,\" his former competitor Bruno Horgan, whose company was caught using sub-standard materials.\n\nThe flashback over the next page shows how he developed his new device to disintegrate metal.&nbsp; As it explicitly mentions that it happened at his lab that was \"built of inferior parts,\" I very much suspect that this will turn out to be the Melter's downfall, but we'll see...\n\nHe melts the inventory that Stark was inspecting and leaves him lying there, so that he can toy with him some more.&nbsp; When he later sneaks into Stark's power plant, his entrance shows up on Stark's alarm board, and Iron Man takes the scene!&nbsp; Meanwhile, the Melter destroys the main generators.&nbsp; When Iron Man confronts him, the Melter destroys the armor on his left arm.&nbsp; Iron Man is forced to flee or risk his chest device being damaged!&nbsp; He drives the Melter away by forcing hot steam into the area before he can do any more damage.\n\nBut Stark is faced with major repairs and an inability to face his foe without being melted away!&nbsp; He leaves orders not to be disturbed.&nbsp; We see Happy and Pepper flirting as they deal with his absence.&nbsp; The government is threatening to cancel all their military contracts if he doesn't solve his problems with the tanks.&nbsp; And on cue, the Melter shows up at his Flushing, Long Island factory.&nbsp; As Iron Man tries to repair the damage, the Melter shows up again!&nbsp; \"This is the <i>showdown</i>, mister!\" Iron Man exclaims!\n\nAnd sure enough, when the Melter tries to attack Iron Man again, his armor doesn't melt!&nbsp; He runs for it, but he's at Stark's plant, so Iron Man knows how to use things around him to cut him off, but the Melter escapes by melting through the floor and diving down into the \"underground disposal system\" and is washed away, leaving Iron Man to wonder if he drowned.&nbsp; (<i>Hey, isn't it at least fair to ask why the floor was made entirely of iron or steel?&nbsp; Was there no wood or concrete present at all?</i>)\n\nThen Iron Man turns to face the reader and informs us that the Melter wasn't really facing Iron Man since he redesigned his armor, \"making it entirely out of tough extruded aluminum!\"&nbsp; Then he returns to the plant as Stark and orders everyone to get back to work!",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "I'll say this for the tale -- it was <i>certainly</i> better than what we've seen from this magazine in recent months.&nbsp; I found myself wondering how Iron Man would defeat the Melter and had no idea why his armor didn't melt.&nbsp; The cheesy Cary Bates Flash-like address to the reader made me laugh briefly (I actually was a fan of that era's Flash and always got a kick out of how everyone would talk directly to the reader.)&nbsp; And I was wrong about my suspicion that the inferior parts might play a role in the Melter's downfall.&nbsp; Oh well.\n\nBut the fact that I wasn't having to force myself to endure yet one more excruciating page of story was <i>definitely</i> a good sign.&nbsp; It certainly wasn't a terrific tale or anything of the sort and doesn't even get a full mid-grade, but it was for <i>sure</i> better than \"reader\" level.&nbsp; The one thing I didn't think was addressed was whether the Melter's weapon was heat-based, which I'm guessing it was <i>not</i>, or else Tony Stark's left arm would have been exposed to molten iron and be scalded, but he seemed not to feel any physical discomfort at all.&nbsp; The Melter uses the term \"disintegrate\" instead, so this must be some kind of heatless \"melting.\"\n\nThe Melter points out early on that Iron Man must work for Stark.&nbsp; I think this might actually be the first time that has been suggested.&nbsp; I can't remember if anything more has ever been revealed other than that Tony Stark <i>knows</i> Iron Man.\n\nHappy and Pepper seem to be in every other panel in this story, as if forcefully squirted into the plot merely to provide for a way to avoid additional narration.&nbsp; It seemed a bit much to me that they happened to be witnesses to <i>everything</i> that was going on.&nbsp; They're even on the scene during the final confrontation, causing Iron Man to have to briefly pause to save them from a melted crane.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nBoth the cover and splash page say that this is a Lee, Ditko and Heck classic, but a mere glance at the artwork reveals that it was drawn by Heck and <i>not</i> Ditko, so perhaps Ditko did the script?&nbsp; If Ditko did indeed do the pencils and Heck the inking, there's <i>absolutely</i> no evidence of it.&nbsp; Ditko's styling of blocking characters, splayed-out fingers, and facial close-ups is not present at <i>all</i> -- this seemed all Heck to me!\n\nAnd heh, as for the back-up story \"Shock!\" -- even though it was <i>painfully</i> obvious early on how it would end, I still got a kick out of that final panel!&nbsp; <i>Hahahahaha</i>!",
  "ownership": "As for me, I got a VG/FN copy as part of the 2000 collection, for which I paid about $21, selling it on eBay in March 2021 for $134, netting me over $95 -- <i>nice</i>!",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}